<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medjerda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bagradas, Medjerda River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Medjerda, the ancient Bagradas, is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, rising in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and flowing about 450 km to the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Its valley was the grain land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In antiquity it reached the sea near Utica, but the silt it carried has since pushed the coast outward, leaving Utica's ruins well inland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medjerda.jpg" alt="Medjerda" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DrFO.Jr.Tn · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medjerda_(RN_5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
